CH = dict(num=3, tag="Geography", theme="Plate tectonics, weathering, erosion, landforms and landform disasters",
 title="Shaping the Earth's Surface",
 intro="The Earth's surface is never still. Forces from deep inside the planet push up mountains and shake the ground, while forces on the surface wear them down and rebuild them elsewhere. This chapter explains the theory of plate tectonics, the work of weathering and erosion, the landforms made by water, waves, ice, wind and groundwater, and the disasters linked to them.",
 big_questions=["What shapes the Earth's surface?", "What is plate tectonics, and what are the effects of plate movement?", "How are landforms formed and classified?", "How are humans and other living beings connected to landforms?", "How do disasters linked to different landforms affect human lives?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Landforms and Plate Tectonics"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **landform** is a natural feature of the Earth's surface made by processes such as weathering, erosion, deposition and movements of the Earth. Examples: mountains, valleys, plateaus, plains, deserts, coastal features."),
 ("p", "**Plate tectonics**, a theory associated with **W.J. Morgan**, explains the movement of the Earth's crust. The outermost layer is not one piece but is broken into large and small **tectonic plates**, which move slowly over the semi-molten layer below. They are responsible for mountains, earthquakes and volcanoes, and explain the distribution of continents and oceans."),
 ("h3", "Interior of the Earth"),
 ("table", ["Layer", "Features"], [
  ["**Crust**", "Outermost solid layer on which we live. Thickness: 30–40 km under continents (continental crust, about 30 km in the figure), 5–7 km under oceans (oceanic crust)"],
  ["**Lithosphere**", "Rigid outer layer: the crust plus the upper part of the mantle (about 100 km). It is broken into tectonic plates"],
  ["**Asthenosphere**", "Hot, mobile, partially molten layer (about 200 km) beneath the lithosphere that allows plates to move"],
  ["**Mantle**", "Very thick, hot, mostly solid layer between crust and core (about 2,900 km)"],
  ["**Outer core**", "Fluid layer mainly of iron and nickel (about 2,200 km)"],
  ["**Inner core**", "Solid, hot, spinning metal ball; the densest part of the Earth (about 1,250 km)"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "All values are approximate. Move down the layers: crust → mantle → outer core → inner core."),
 ("h3", "Plates and why they move"),
 ("ul", ["Tectonic plates are huge slabs of solid rock that move only **a few centimetres a year**.", "Types: **continental** plates (carry continents), **oceanic** plates (carry ocean floors) and **mixed** plates (carry both).", "Major plates: Pacific, Eurasian, African, North American, South American, Indo-Australian and Antarctic; there are also minor plates.", "**Cause:** heat from the core makes hot material in the mantle rise while cooler material sinks. These **convection currents** push and pull the plates in different directions."]),
 ("h3", "Plate boundaries"),
 ("table", ["Boundary", "Plate movement", "Result", "Example"], [
  ["**Convergent**", "Plates move towards each other", "Continent–continent collision forms **fold mountains**; oceanic plate sinks beneath a continental plate, causing volcanoes and earthquakes", "Himalaya"],
  ["**Divergent**", "Plates move apart", "Magma rises and forms new crust, making **mid-ocean ridges**", "Mid-Atlantic Ridge"],
  ["**Transform**", "Plates slide past each other", "No crust created or destroyed; mainly earthquakes", "San Andreas Fault, USA"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Most earthquakes and volcanoes occur along plate boundaries, especially around the Pacific Ocean, an area called the **Ring of Fire**. Comparing the plate map with the earthquake and volcano map shows this link. The theory helps identify earthquake and volcano-prone regions."),
 ("h3", "Earthquakes and volcanoes: India and the past"),
 ("ul", ["India has had major earthquakes with thousands of deaths, such as the **Gujarat earthquake of 2001**. Dense population makes damage severe.", "Earthquakes were known in early India as **bhūkampa** ('shaking of the Earth'). **Varāhamihira**, in the **Bṛihatsaṁhitā**, noted that changes in wind, rain, clouds, animal behaviour and planetary alignments could signal them, and linked them to four elemental forces: Vāyu (wind), Agni (fire), Indra (heaven/thunder) and Varuṇa (water). It was an early blend of observation and cosmological reasoning.", "Volcanic eruptions deposit grey ash, showing the Earth's internal forces at work.", "**Baratang Island** (Andaman and Nicobar Islands) has India's only **mud volcano**: mud bubbles out due to underground gases and pressure instead of fiery lava."]),
 ("h2", "Weathering and Erosion"),
 ("p", "Together, weathering and erosion wear down mountains, carve valleys, form plains and create caves, cliffs and deltas."),
 ("table", ["", "Weathering", "Erosion"], [
  ["Meaning", "Breaking down of rocks into smaller pieces at the surface", "Wearing away and **carrying** of soil and rock from one place to another"],
  ["Movement of material", "No movement", "Involves movement by agents: water, wind, ice, waves"],
  ["Types", "Physical, chemical, biological", "Water, wind, glacial, coastal"]]),
 ("table", ["Type of weathering", "Cause", "Example"], [
  ["**Physical**", "Temperature change (heating and cooling expansion), frost, wind", "Rocks crack into smaller pieces"],
  ["**Chemical**", "Minerals react with water, air or acids to form new substances", "Rain and air altering rock minerals"],
  ["**Biological**", "Plants, animals, micro-organisms", "Plant roots growing into cracks and splitting rock"]]),
 ("p", "Weathering also forms **soil**. Types of erosion: **water** (rivers, rain, waves), **wind** (dry, sandy areas), **glacial** (moving ice scrapes and carries rocks) and **coastal** (sea waves wear the shore)."),
 ("h3", "Erosion and human livelihoods"),
 ("ul", ["**Farmers:** fertile topsoil is removed, so yields fall.", "**River and coastal dwellers:** land, houses and roads are washed away.", "**Construction and mining:** unstable land creates safety risks.", "**Tourism and fishing:** beaches, rivers and fertile lands are destroyed."]),
 ("table", ["Soil conservation method", "Meaning"], [
  ["**Contouring** (continuous contour trench)", "Trenches dug along contour lines of a hillside to slow water, let rain soak in, prevent erosion and recharge groundwater"],
  ["**Bunding**", "Earthen embankments along contour lines to slow run-off, reduce erosion and increase infiltration and soil moisture"],
  ["**Terracing**", "Level or gently sloping steps cut on a hillside to prevent soil erosion"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "The **Sindhu-Sarasvatī civilisation** used contouring, bunding, terracing, dams and canals. Texts such as the Vedas, Kṛiṣhiparāśhara, Kauṭilya's Arthaśhāstra (land assessment by fertility) and Vṛikṣhāyurveda record such practices. The **Zabo system** of Nagaland uses earthen bunds on hillslopes for soil and water conservation; **check dams** across small streams slow water, prevent erosion, let sediment settle and recharge groundwater."),
 ("h2", "Agents of Gradation"),
 ("p", "**Agents of gradation** wear down, transport and deposit material, levelling the surface over time: **running water, glaciers, wind, waves and groundwater**. They lower high areas and fill low areas."),
 ("table", ["Agent", "Main work"], [
  ["Running water", "Erodes rocks and soil, forming valleys and plains"],
  ["Glaciers", "Scrape and carry huge amounts of material; carve U-shaped valleys"],
  ["Wind", "Shapes deserts by eroding and depositing sand"],
  ["Sea waves", "Erode coasts to form cliffs, beaches, bays"],
  ["Groundwater", "Dissolves limestone, creating caves and sinkholes"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Landforms have shaped history. Rivers and plains (Ganga, Nile, Brahmaputra, Indus) gave rise to farming societies and cities. The Himalayas shielded India yet allowed exchange through passes like the **Khyber Pass**. Deserts like the **Thar** limited large settlements but encouraged trade routes like the **Silk Route**. Coasts and harbours supported trade and kingdoms in south India."),
 ("h3", "Running water"),
 ("table", ["Course of river", "Process", "Landforms"], [
  ["**Upper**", "Steep gradient, strong erosion", "V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, rapids"],
  ["**Middle**", "Loses energy, begins to deposit", "Meanders, oxbow lakes, floodplains"],
  ["**Lower**", "Slows further, heavy deposition", "Deltas, levees, alluvial fans"]]),
 ("table", ["Landform", "Formation", "Human importance"], [
  ["**Waterfall**", "River falls over a steep drop in the upper course where hard rock resists erosion and softer rock below is worn away", "Tourism, hydroelectric power, trekking and photography, religious and cultural value"],
  ["**Meander**", "Winding bend of middle/lower course: river erodes the outer bank and deposits sediment on the inner bank; a cut-off loop becomes an **oxbow lake**", "Fertile soil for farming, settlements on gentle slopes, navigation, irrigation, tourism. Example: Grand Anicut (Kallanai), Tamil Nadu, irrigation"],
  ["**Delta**", "At the river mouth where it meets sea/lake, sediment builds up into a fan-shaped or triangular area, with distributaries", "Very fertile (rice, jute), fishing, dense settlement, trade routes; but flood-prone. Example: Sundarbans delta"]]),
 ("h3", "Waves and currents (coasts)"),
 ("ul", ["Waves and currents create beaches, sand bars, sea cliffs, caves, arches and stacks.", "A **beach** is a deposit of sand, pebbles or rock along a shore. It supports tourism, fishing, shell and sand collection and acts as a natural barrier against waves and erosion."]),
 ("table", ["Coastal erosion landform", "Meaning"], [["Cliff", "Steep rock face formed as waves undercut the base"], ["Wave-cut (shore) platform", "Flat area left as cliffs retreat"], ["Cave", "Formed as waves erode weak parts of rock"], ["Arch", "Caves on opposite sides of a headland meet"], ["Stack", "Isolated rock pillar left when the arch collapses"]]),
 ("h3", "Glaciers"),
 ("table", ["Landform", "Meaning"], [["**U-shaped valley**", "River valley widened and deepened by a glacier"], ["**Cirque**", "Bowl-shaped hollow at the head of a glacier"], ["**Arête**", "Sharp ridge between valleys"], ["**Hanging valley**", "Where a smaller glacier meets a larger one"], ["**Fjord**", "Deep, narrow sea inlet formed when the sea floods a glacial valley"]]),
 ("p", "Human use: U-shaped valleys and cirques for trekking, skiing, mountaineering; fjords for harbours and fishing; fertile glacial soil; glaciers as sources of fresh water feeding rivers."),
 ("table", ["Moraine (deposited debris called till)", "Where formed"], [["**Lateral**", "Along the sides of a glacier"], ["**Terminal**", "At the end, marking the furthest advance"], ["**Medial**", "In the middle, when two glaciers meet and their lateral moraines join"]]),
 ("p", "Moraines give fertile soil and can form natural dams and lakes for water supply, irrigation and hydroelectric power. Case: the **Chamoli flood, Uttarakhand, February 2021**, killed people and livestock and damaged buildings, roads, bridges and hydel projects."),
 ("h3", "Wind"),
 ("table", ["Landform", "Meaning"], [["**Yardang**", "Streamlined rock ridge carved by wind"], ["**Ventifact**", "Rock polished and shaped by sandblasting"], ["**Deflation hollow (blowout)**", "Shallow depression where loose material is blown away (may expose water table: oasis)"], ["**Desert pavement**", "Flat surface left after fine particles are blown away"]]),
 ("table", ["Dune type", "Features"], [["**Barchan**", "Crescent-shaped; limited sand, single wind direction"], ["**Longitudinal**", "Long ridges parallel to prevailing wind"], ["**Star**", "Many arms; winds from different directions"], ["**Parabolic**", "U-shaped, often stabilised by vegetation"]]),
 ("p", "Dunes act as barriers against desertification and wind erosion, protect coastal settlements from sea winds, give adventure sports and supply construction sand."),
 ("h3", "Underground water (karst topography)"),
 ("p", "In limestone and other soluble rocks, acidic water dissolves rock by chemical weathering and erosion, giving **karst topography**."),
 ("table", ["Landform", "Meaning"], [["**Cave**", "Hollow space dissolved by acidic water"], ["**Stalactite**", "Icicle-shaped formation hanging from the cave ceiling"], ["**Stalagmite**", "Formation rising from the cave floor"], ["**Sinkhole (doline)**", "Depression where ground collapses into an underground cavity"], ["**Underground river**", "Flows through cave systems"]]),
 ("p", "Caves and underground rivers provide fresh water, tourism, cultural or religious significance; stalactites and stalagmites attract geologists and adventurers."),
 ("h2", "Landforms and Disasters"),
 ("table", ["Disaster", "Causes", "Human factors / notes"], [
  ["**Landslide**", "Heavy continuous rain (adds weight, reduces friction); earthquakes and eruptions; steep slopes; loose or weathered rock", "Deforestation, mining, road building, unplanned hillside construction, poor drainage and improper land use"],
  ["**Avalanche**", "Heavy snowfall in a short time; weak snow layers; sudden temperature rise (melting reduces friction); strong winds piling snow; earthquakes and vibrations", "Skiing, trekking, construction in mountains"],
  ["**GLOF** (Glacial Lake Outburst Flood)", "Rapid glacier melt from rising temperatures strains ice/moraine dams; heavy rain or snow; earthquakes, avalanches or landslides weaken the dam, releasing water suddenly", "Destructive floods downstream (compare Chamoli 2021)"],
  ["**Dust storm**", "Strong winds lift loose dry soil; prolonged drought; desert and semi-arid regions; sparse vegetation", "Deforestation, overgrazing, poor farming; climate change raises frequency"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "For each disaster, be ready to list prone areas, mitigation measures and recent examples from India and the world. Mitigation includes afforestation, better drainage and land-use planning, early warning, monitoring glacial lakes, and awareness and preparedness drills."),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Internal (endogenic) forces**: earthquakes, volcanoes, folding and faulting build mountains, valleys and ocean basins. **External (exogenic) forces**: weathering, erosion and deposition wear them down and reshape them."),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is plate tectonics?", "A theory (W.J. Morgan) that the crust is broken into plates that move slowly over the semi-molten asthenosphere, forming mountains, volcanoes and earthquakes."),
  ("Describe the layers of the Earth.", "Crust, mantle (with the asthenosphere below the lithosphere), outer core (liquid iron-nickel), inner core (solid, densest)."),
  ("What causes plate movement?", "Convection currents in the mantle driven by heat from the core."),
  ("Name and explain the three plate boundaries.", "Convergent (move together; Himalaya), divergent (move apart; Mid-Atlantic Ridge), transform (slide past; San Andreas Fault)."),
  ("What is the Ring of Fire?", "Zone around the Pacific Ocean with most earthquakes and volcanoes, along plate boundaries."),
  ("Differentiate weathering from erosion.", "Weathering breaks rock in place; erosion removes and transports material."),
  ("Name the types of weathering.", "Physical, chemical and biological."),
  ("How does erosion affect livelihoods? Name soil conservation methods.", "Farmers lose topsoil, coasts lose land, etc. Methods: contouring, bunding, terracing, check dams."),
  ("List the agents of gradation.", "Running water, glaciers, wind, waves, groundwater."),
  ("Explain how a delta and a meander form, and why they matter.", "Delta: sediment deposition at river mouth; meander: erosion on outer bank, deposition on inner. Both give fertile land but delta is flood-prone."),
  ("Name landforms made by glaciers and by wind.", "Glacial: U-valley, cirque, arête, hanging valley, fjord, moraines. Wind: yardang, ventifact, deflation hollow, desert pavement, dunes."),
  ("What are karst landforms?", "Caves, stalactites, stalagmites, sinkholes and underground rivers formed by dissolving of limestone."),
  ("Explain GLOFs.", "Sudden floods when glacial lake dams of ice or moraine fail due to melting, heavy rain or shocks."),
  ("How do human activities cause landslides?", "Deforestation, mining, road building, unplanned construction and poor drainage."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["The Earth has crust, mantle and core; the lithosphere is broken into tectonic plates that move by mantle convection.", "Plate boundaries (convergent, divergent, transform) explain mountains, ridges, earthquakes and volcanoes, concentrated in the Ring of Fire.", "Weathering breaks rock; erosion carries it away; deposition builds new landforms.", "Running water, waves, glaciers, wind and groundwater are the agents of gradation, each with characteristic landforms.", "Landforms shape settlement, farming, trade, tourism and history, while landslides, avalanches, GLOFs and dust storms threaten life.", "Soil conservation (contouring, bunding, terracing) and preparedness reduce damage."])
